1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,486
In a village in eastern China there was a pond the locals called bottomless.

2
00:00:05,486 --> 00:00:08,130
In the worst droughts it never dropped.

3
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:10,300
Nobody had ever seen the bottom.

4
00:00:10,766 --> 00:00:15,556
In June nineteen ninety-two, four farmers decided to find out why.

5
00:00:15,556 --> 00:00:17,443
They brought a water pump.

6
00:00:17,443 --> 00:00:19,330
They ran it day and night.

7
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:20,999
It took seventeen days.

8
00:00:21,466 --> 00:00:24,379
What the water had hidden was not a pond.

9
00:00:24,379 --> 00:00:35,533
It was the roof of a hall carved into solid rock, as tall as a ten-storey building, with giant pillars and every wall covered in neat, parallel chisel marks.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,764
And because no history book seems to mention it, a question has followed it ever since.

11
00:00:41,764 --> 00:00:45,673
Who could cut a cave like this, and leave no record at all?

12
00:00:45,673 --> 00:00:46,799
Some say: not us.

13
00:00:47,266 --> 00:00:51,729
So today we drain this case the way the farmers drained the pond.

14
00:00:51,729 --> 00:00:52,759
Level by level.

15
00:00:52,759 --> 00:00:58,732
At each new waterline we look at what has been uncovered: the evidence, and the theory.

16
00:00:58,732 --> 00:01:01,066
At the floor, we give the verdict.

17
00:01:05,533 --> 00:01:07,079
Level one: the pond.

18
00:01:07,079 --> 00:01:14,966
The village is Shiyanbei, on Fenghuang Hill, beside the Qu River in Longyou County, Zhejiang province.

19
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:19,970
The village had several of these small, square, stone-edged pools.

20
00:01:19,970 --> 00:01:23,407
Villagers drew water from them and fished in them.

21
00:01:23,407 --> 00:01:25,606
Some had fish, others never did.

22
00:01:25,606 --> 00:01:28,700
That oddity is what made the farmers curious.

23
00:01:29,166 --> 00:01:33,318
A local farmer, Wu Anai, and three companions started pumping.

24
00:01:33,318 --> 00:01:36,733
One story says they hoped for a tourist attraction.

25
00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:44,033
Another says they were looking for a jade statue rumoured to have been thrown into the water decades earlier.

26
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,552
As the water dropped, the sides did not stop.

27
00:01:47,552 --> 00:01:52,776
They sloped inward, and a stone staircase appeared, cut from the living rock.

28
00:01:52,776 --> 00:01:56,099
After seventeen days the pumps reached the floor.

29
00:01:56,566 --> 00:02:01,784
Underneath was a cavern with a sloping roof, stone pillars and a flat floor.

30
00:02:01,784 --> 00:02:04,256
They pumped four neighbouring ponds.

31
00:02:04,256 --> 00:02:07,071
Each one was the doorway to another hall.

32
00:02:07,071 --> 00:02:10,366
Later, nineteen more chambers were found nearby.

33
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:13,820
The five drained caves are now a tourist site.

34
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:23,366
The biggest single chambers cover well over a thousand square metres, and the deepest drops more than thirty metres from the entrance to the floor.

35
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:27,967
So the caves are real, they are man-made, and they are huge.

36
00:02:27,967 --> 00:02:29,897
That is the first waterline.

37
00:02:29,897 --> 00:02:31,000
Now, the claims.

38
00:02:31,466 --> 00:02:33,047
Level two: the claims.

39
00:02:33,047 --> 00:02:39,800
The caves became a staple of mystery websites, and of the Ancient Aliens television franchise.

40
00:02:40,266 --> 00:02:42,614
The argument starts with the walls.

41
00:02:42,614 --> 00:02:51,601
Across thousands of square metres, the chisel marks run in even bands about sixty centimetres wide, each stroke set at the same angle.

42
00:02:51,601 --> 00:02:56,833
To some eyes, that looks like the cutting head of a machine, not a human hand.

43
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:03,508
Online, people compare the pattern to the marks left by modern tunnel-boring machines.

44
00:03:03,508 --> 00:03:09,500
Some websites go further and credit the Anunnaki, or a lost high-tech civilisation.

45
00:03:09,966 --> 00:03:11,131
Then the numbers.

46
00:03:11,131 --> 00:03:18,053
Estimates for the stone removed from the known caves run to about nine hundred thousand cubic metres.

47
00:03:18,053 --> 00:03:19,766
Where did all of that go?

48
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:21,568
Then the precision.

49
00:03:21,568 --> 00:03:29,511
In places, two caves are separated by a wall only about half a metre thick, yet they never break into each other.

50
00:03:29,511 --> 00:03:32,533
How did ancient builders survey that blind?

51
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,261
Then the darkness.

52
00:03:34,261 --> 00:03:40,007
Popular accounts say no soot from lamps or torches has been found on the ceilings.

53
00:03:40,007 --> 00:03:41,900
So how did the workers see?

54
00:03:42,366 --> 00:03:47,058
And the heart of the claim: China kept records for thousands of years.

55
00:03:47,058 --> 00:03:51,213
A project this big, the argument goes, should be in the books.

56
00:03:51,213 --> 00:03:51,816
It isn't.

57
00:03:51,816 --> 00:03:54,966
Therefore someone, or something, else built it.

58
00:03:55,433 --> 00:04:02,434
Those are the claims: machine-like marks, missing stone, impossible precision, no light, no record.

59
00:04:02,434 --> 00:04:03,990
The water drops again.

60
00:04:03,990 --> 00:04:05,900
Let's test them one by one.

61
00:04:06,366 --> 00:04:07,894
Level three: the marks.

62
00:04:07,894 --> 00:04:10,816
Look closely and the pattern is not perfect.

63
00:04:10,816 --> 00:04:13,007
Strokes vary in length and depth.

64
00:04:13,007 --> 00:04:13,870
Bands wander.

65
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:23,500
An observer who visited in the year two thousand made exactly that point: these are the small differences of skilled craftsmen, not of a machine.

66
00:04:23,966 --> 00:04:26,503
Then the tools themselves turned up.

67
00:04:26,503 --> 00:04:35,803
In January two thousand and two, clearing the entrance of cave two, workers found an iron wedge still jammed in a crack in the rock.

68
00:04:35,803 --> 00:04:38,833
Masons call this kind of wedge a stone rat.

69
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:47,793
Three days later, in the silt of cave five, they found a short iron chisel with a square point and a thick spindle-shaped body.

70
00:04:47,793 --> 00:04:49,933
A quarryman's tool, not a laser.

71
00:04:50,399 --> 00:05:02,543
In two thousand and seven, for a China Central Television documentary, Professor Zhou Shaoxiong asked local stonemasons to show how they still cut this rock underground.

72
00:05:02,543 --> 00:05:05,633
The method has a local name: the earth rat.

73
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:11,321
The masons cut a channel, drove wedges, and peeled the stone off from above.

74
00:05:11,321 --> 00:05:18,466
What they left behind were rows of regular, slanted chisel marks with the same rhythm as the cave walls.

75
00:05:18,933 --> 00:05:20,917
The same team tested samples.

76
00:05:20,917 --> 00:05:26,594
Rock taken from underground was much softer than the weathered rock at the surface.

77
00:05:26,594 --> 00:05:32,066
So working down into the hill, out of the sun, was actually easier on the tools.

78
00:05:32,533 --> 00:05:33,618
Why so regular?

79
00:05:33,618 --> 00:05:40,850
Because a mason working a soft, even rock, strike after strike, in a set rhythm, produces a pattern.

80
00:05:40,850 --> 00:05:42,948
Repetition is not technology.

81
00:05:42,948 --> 00:05:44,033
It is practice.

82
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:49,706
Iron wedge, iron chisel, and a living craft that reproduces the marks.

83
00:05:49,706 --> 00:05:53,276
The machine theory just lost its strongest clue.

84
00:05:53,276 --> 00:05:54,466
The water drops.

85
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:56,419
Level four: the plan.

86
00:05:56,419 --> 00:06:00,595
If the marks show human hands, the shapes show human heads.

87
00:06:00,595 --> 00:06:05,833
Each cave keeps one to five huge pillars, left standing in the solid rock.

88
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:13,915
They are an odd shape: wide at one end, curved and narrow at the other, like a fish tail or an old flat iron.

89
00:06:13,915 --> 00:06:20,133
The long side runs along the slope of the roof, the direction where the load is greatest.

90
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:21,950
And the roofs slope.

91
00:06:21,950 --> 00:06:31,945
Engineers from the Chinese Academy of Sciences measured them and found the slope of each ceiling almost matches the tilt of the natural rock layers.

92
00:06:31,945 --> 00:06:36,199
Cut along the bedding, and the roof is far less likely to fall.

93
00:06:36,666 --> 00:06:39,185
Even better, there are trial tunnels.

94
00:06:39,185 --> 00:06:45,584
Small test adits, started and then abandoned where a weak layer of clay showed up in the rock.

95
00:06:45,584 --> 00:06:52,800
A twenty sixteen study calls this probably the earliest known use of exploratory adits to test the ground.

96
00:06:53,266 --> 00:06:53,866
Lighting?

97
00:06:53,866 --> 00:07:00,000
The entrances face roughly south, so sunlight falls deep into the halls for much of the day.

98
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:07,733
And small oil lamps or clean-burning torches leave little soot, especially on walls that spent centuries underwater.

99
00:07:08,199 --> 00:07:09,458
And the thin walls?

100
00:07:09,458 --> 00:07:20,719
Masons cutting downward from the surface can measure from the openings they can see, and a skilled crew working slowly does not need to break through to know where it is.

101
00:07:20,719 --> 00:07:22,442
A thin wall is impressive.

102
00:07:22,442 --> 00:07:23,833
It is not impossible.

103
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:26,628
Look at the floors and stairs too.

104
00:07:26,628 --> 00:07:31,147
The steps are often two or three metres high, useless for walking.

105
00:07:31,147 --> 00:07:38,199
But they look exactly like quarry benches: the stepped faces left when stone is cut out layer by layer.

106
00:07:38,666 --> 00:07:43,158
So the caves were planned by people who read rock like a book.

107
00:07:43,158 --> 00:07:46,999
Pillars, sloping roofs, test tunnels, quarry benches.

108
00:07:46,999 --> 00:07:49,172
Brilliant, and entirely human.

109
00:07:49,172 --> 00:07:50,766
The water drops again.

110
00:07:51,233 --> 00:07:53,354
Level five: the missing stone.

111
00:07:53,354 --> 00:07:57,030
Nine hundred thousand cubic metres is a lot of rock.

112
00:07:57,030 --> 00:08:04,100
But look where the caves are: right on the bank of the Qu River, a navigable branch of the Qiantang.

113
00:08:04,566 --> 00:08:08,893
If this was a quarry, the stone was the product, not the waste.

114
00:08:08,893 --> 00:08:10,817
You would not expect a heap.

115
00:08:10,817 --> 00:08:15,899
You would expect it in walls, foundations, bridges and pagodas downstream.

116
00:08:16,366 --> 00:08:19,000
And that is what researchers report.

117
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:30,266
Local studies describe a long tradition of quarrying this red sandstone across Longyou County, used above all for the bases of walls, pagodas and bridges.

118
00:08:30,733 --> 00:08:42,933
Curators at the Quzhou museum surveyed similar stone chambers across the region and argued the same thing: these were quarries, and the stone went into city walls and other big works.

119
00:08:43,399 --> 00:08:45,001
There is a sister site.

120
00:08:45,001 --> 00:08:56,000
Two hundred kilometres north-west, on the Xin'an River, the Huashan Grottoes have the same pillars, the same banded chisel marks, the same riverside position.

121
00:08:56,466 --> 00:09:06,931
Scientists dated the Huashan caves by measuring lichen growth on their walls, and the answer came back as the late Ming dynasty, a few centuries ago.

122
00:09:06,931 --> 00:09:13,533
A comparative study concluded both groups were building-stone quarries along navigable rivers.

123
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,160
Why dig down instead of opening the hillside?

124
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:26,218
Because the softer underground rock is easier to cut, the good beds run downward at an angle, and farmland on top stays farmland.

125
00:09:26,218 --> 00:09:31,766
Even the discoverers objected to the quarry idea, but the geology answers them.

126
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:34,611
The missing stone is not missing.

127
00:09:34,611 --> 00:09:40,447
It most likely floated away, block by block, and became the region's foundations.

128
00:09:40,447 --> 00:09:41,599
The water drops.

129
00:09:42,066 --> 00:09:43,423
Level six: the date.

130
00:09:43,423 --> 00:09:46,205
And this is where the real mystery lives.

131
00:09:46,205 --> 00:09:47,833
Not who could, but when.

132
00:09:48,299 --> 00:09:57,437
When silt was cleared from cave two in two thousand and one, diggers found pieces of glazed pottery identified as Han dynasty.

133
00:09:57,437 --> 00:10:03,166
That is why so many articles say the caves are at least two thousand years old.

134
00:10:03,633 --> 00:10:07,357
But pottery in the mud dates the mud, not the cutting.

135
00:10:07,357 --> 00:10:14,666
And the same layers also held Ming porcelain and modern rubbish, washed in after the caves were abandoned.

136
00:10:15,133 --> 00:10:22,950
In two thousand and eight, the Zhejiang Provincial Institute of Archaeology excavated caves six and seven.

137
00:10:22,950 --> 00:10:29,366
They found jars of a type known from Song dynasty tombs, and Ming blue-and-white bowls.

138
00:10:29,833 --> 00:10:34,291
A panel of six senior national experts then inspected the caves.

139
00:10:34,291 --> 00:10:43,766
Their preliminary view: the caves were cut around the Song dynasty, roughly a thousand years ago, and caves six and seven were a quarry.

140
00:10:44,233 --> 00:10:51,110
Other scholars still argue for older dates, from the Han, or even the age of the Yue kingdom.

141
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:54,512
Proposals span more than three thousand years.

142
00:10:54,512 --> 00:10:57,766
Rock carved by hand gives no carbon to date.

143
00:10:58,233 --> 00:11:02,311
So the honest answer is: we do not yet know exactly when.

144
00:11:02,311 --> 00:11:07,033
Perhaps one long quarry, worked and abandoned over many centuries.

145
00:11:07,033 --> 00:11:12,399
That is a real, open question, and a much more interesting one than aliens.

146
00:11:12,866 --> 00:11:14,643
The water is almost gone.

147
00:11:14,643 --> 00:11:16,633
One level left: the silence.

148
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:18,850
Level seven: the silence.

149
00:11:18,850 --> 00:11:22,000
Is it true that no record mentions the caves?

150
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:22,700
Not quite.

151
00:11:23,166 --> 00:11:34,313
A poem from the Yuan dynasty, about a riverside cavern in this same cluster, says that for ten thousand ages the marks of axes and chisels still remain.

152
00:11:34,313 --> 00:11:38,933
A seventeenth-century poem by Yu Xun mentions the chambers too.

153
00:11:39,399 --> 00:11:52,899
The county's gazetteers, the official local histories printed in sixteen twelve, sixteen eighty-one and nineteen twenty-five, include writing on Fenghuang Hill and describe riverside quarry caves.

154
00:11:53,366 --> 00:11:58,566
What is missing is a building record: who ordered the work, who paid, who cut.

155
00:11:58,566 --> 00:12:00,700
And that is normal for quarries.

156
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:07,100
Chroniclers wrote about emperors, wars and temples, not about the men who cut foundation stones.

157
00:12:07,566 --> 00:12:16,141
After they were abandoned, the flooded caves became village ponds, and one was even used as a temple fish-release pool.

158
00:12:16,141 --> 00:12:17,366
The workers left.

159
00:12:17,366 --> 00:12:18,663
The water came in.

160
00:12:18,663 --> 00:12:19,600
Memory faded.

161
00:12:20,066 --> 00:12:22,633
Ironically, the water also saved them.

162
00:12:22,633 --> 00:12:28,103
Engineers found that being full of water kept the soft rock stable for centuries.

163
00:12:28,103 --> 00:12:35,533
Since the pumps ran, cracks and small rockfalls have appeared, and steel supports were added in twenty twelve.

164
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,142
So the silence was never total.

165
00:12:38,142 --> 00:12:45,466
It was the ordinary silence of working people, whose labour fills our cities but rarely our history books.

166
00:12:45,933 --> 00:12:46,814
The pumps stop.

167
00:12:46,814 --> 00:12:48,341
We have reached the floor.

168
00:12:48,341 --> 00:12:49,633
Here is the scorecard.

169
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:54,133
An iron wedge and an iron chisel in the caves: evidence.

170
00:12:54,133 --> 00:12:57,735
Chisel marks reproduced by local masons: evidence.

171
00:12:57,735 --> 00:13:02,057
Roofs cut along the rock layers, and test tunnels: evidence.

172
00:13:02,057 --> 00:13:07,100
The missing stone, shipped down the river into local building: likely.

173
00:13:07,566 --> 00:13:11,768
Alien machines or lost high technology: no evidence.

174
00:13:11,768 --> 00:13:15,404
A secret army base or royal palace: unproven.

175
00:13:15,404 --> 00:13:20,333
The exact date and first purpose of the caves: still debated.

176
00:13:20,799 --> 00:13:21,647
The verdict.

177
00:13:21,647 --> 00:13:31,112
The Longyou Caves were cut by people, with iron tools, by masons who understood their rock better than most of us understand anything.

178
00:13:31,112 --> 00:13:36,833
The strongest explanation is a vast building-stone quarry beside a river highway.

179
00:13:37,299 --> 00:13:42,187
What stays open is the date, and whether some halls were later reused.

180
00:13:42,187 --> 00:13:47,633
That is a question for archaeologists with trowels and lichen, not for aliens.

181
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:56,119
And the real wonder is this: a hill hollowed out by hand, stroke by stroke, by people whose names nobody wrote down.

182
00:13:56,119 --> 00:13:57,640
They are the builders.

183
00:13:57,640 --> 00:13:59,299
They deserve the credit.

184
00:13:59,766 --> 00:14:00,833
That's the floor.

185
00:14:00,833 --> 00:14:03,029
Which waterline surprised you most?

186
00:14:03,029 --> 00:14:08,299
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
